    "evidenceByGoal": "Children with growth hormone deficiency (GHD): randomized dose-escalation trial of escalating single doses of once-weekly somapacitan versus once-daily GH; the study evaluated safety, local tolerability, pharmacodynamics, and pharmacokinetics. [452] Population: children with GHD. [452] Sample size: not verified in reviewed source. Dose: dose amounts not verified in reviewed source; the source describes escalating single doses. [452] Duration: not verified in reviewed source. Comparator: once-daily GH. [452] Outcomes: safety, local tolerability, pharmacodynamics, and pharmacokinetics; outcome findings were not verified in the supplied source. [452] Adverse events: not verified in reviewed source. Key limitation: the reported intervention was single-dose, and other limitations were not verified in reviewed source. [452]\n\nHealthy adults: randomized, double-blind, crossover bioequivalence trial comparing somapacitan 5 mg/1.5 mL with 10 mg/1.5 mL in equimolar doses. [453] Population: 33 healthy adults; eligibility was age 18–45 years and BMI 18.5–24.9 kg/m2. [453] Dose and duration: one injection using the 5 mg pen and two injections using the 10 mg pen, with rounds at least 3 weeks apart. [453] Comparator: the 5 mg/1.5 mL and 10 mg/1.5 mL strengths. [453] Outcomes: AUC0–t, Cmax, time to Cmax, terminal half-life, safety, and IGF-I responses. [453] AUC0–t bioequivalence was met, with ETR 0.95 (90% CI 0.89–1.01), but Cmax bioequivalence was not met, with ETR 0.77 (90% CI 0.68–0.89); the two strengths had equivalent IGF-I responses. [453] Adverse events: the source reports that no new safety issues were identified. [453] Key limitations: the participants were healthy adults rather than patients with GHD, and Cmax bioequivalence was not demonstrated. [453]\n\nThe first candidate record was a model-based analysis of three phase I trials, not a primary human outcome trial, and was therefore not counted as an eligible primary study. [451]",
