    "humanStudyCount": 2,
    "totalStudyCount": 3,
    "evidenceByGoal": "Goal: linear growth in children with pediatric growth hormone deficiency (pGHD), with safety outcomes reported alongside efficacy. Two uniquely identifiable eligible primary human studies were found among the three records reviewed. Record 1 was a randomized phase 3 pediatric study followed by an open-label extension: 224 children were randomized for 12 months to once-weekly somatrogon 0.66 mg/kg or once-daily Genotropin 0.24 mg/kg/week; 212 entered the extension and 161 had complete month-12 extension auxological data. [454] The reported month-12 extension height velocity/change in height SDS were 8.04 cm/year/0.41 for continuous somatrogon and 8.21 cm/year/0.47 for children switched from Genotropin; the main trial reported once-weekly somatrogon as non-inferior to Genotropin. [454] Adverse events were mostly mild to moderate; six switched-group patients discontinued during the first 12 extension months because of adverse events versus none in the continuous-somatrogon group. [454] Key limitation: the extension was open-label and only 161 of 212 extension entrants had complete month-12 auxological data. [454]\n\nRecord 2 reported longer follow-up of the same pivotal trial, identified as NCT02968004, rather than a separate unique study: patients were randomized during the 12-month main study to somatrogon 0.66 mg/kg/week or somatropin 0.24 mg/kg/week, then entered an open-label extension; 222 completed the main study, 212 entered extension year 1, and 177 entered year 2. [455] Through extension year 2, mean height velocity was 8.11 cm/year in year 1 and 7.91 cm/year in year 2, while mean change in height SDS was 1.36 and 1.61, respectively; switchers had similar growth measures to continuous somatrogon recipients. [455] All-causality adverse-event incidence was 71.7% and 72.3% in extension years 1 and 2, mostly mild or moderate; injection-site pain and erythema were the most common treatment-related events, and up to one quarter met protocol criteria for dose reduction. [455] Key limitation: this was an open-label extension report of the same pivotal trial, not an independent randomized study. [455]\n\nRecord 3 was a distinct randomized, open-label, dose-finding phase 2 human study in children with pGHD: subjects received once-weekly somatrogon titrated up to 0.25, 0.48, or 0.66 mg/kg/week or once-daily Genotropin 0.24 mg/kg/week for 12 months, followed by an open-label extension; 53 completed the main study and 48 entered the extension, with treatment continuing up to 8 years overall. [456] At extension year 7, 26 subjects had mean height velocity 5.31 cm/year and mean height SDS 0.44; four subjects achieved final height. [456] In extension year 7, treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 35.5% of subjects, no serious or withdrawal-related events occurred, two subjects had study-drug-related events, and no deaths were reported throughout the extension. [456] Key limitation: the study had a small and declining long-term evaluable sample, from 53 main-study completers and 48 extension entrants to 26 subjects at extension year 7. [456]\n\nNo other clinically studied goal was verified in the reviewed records.",
    "efficacyScore": 3,
    "evidenceQualityScore": 3,
    "safetySummary": "In record 1, adverse events were mostly mild to moderate, with no clinically concerning safety observations; six patients in the switched group discontinued during the first 12 extension months because of adverse events versus none in the continuous-somatrogon group. [454] In record 2, all-causality adverse-event incidence was 71.7% in extension year 1 and 72.3% in year 2, most events were mild or moderate, and injection-site pain and erythema were the most common treatment-related events. [455] In record 3, extension-year-7 treatment-emergent adverse events occurred in 35.5% of subjects; no serious or withdrawal-related events and no deaths were reported in the extension. [456]",
