    "evidenceByGoal": "Human urinary excretion, metabolism, and analytical detection (not therapeutic efficacy): (1) Excretion/endogenous-origin study: design: 5-week in vivo human trial; population/sample: 6 men and 6 women (n=12); dose: daily phytosterol-enriched yogurt drink at the recommended dose, with the exact dose not verified in reviewed source; duration: 5 weeks; comparator: phytosterol-consumption test group versus participants refraining from phytosterol consumption as control; outcome: endogenous boldione/ADD was sporadically detected at 0.751–1.73 ng/mL, endogenous boldenone was not proven, and no relation between ADD excretion and recommended-dose phytosterol consumption was indicated; adverse events: not verified in reviewed source; key limitation: the source describes this as a preliminary in vivo trial and reports urinary analytical outcomes rather than clinical efficacy. [489] (2) Oral dosing/excretion study: design: human urinary excretion study; population/sample: one healthy volunteer; dose: 100 mg boldione orally; duration: boldione and metabolites were detectable in urine for 48 hours; comparator: not verified in reviewed source; outcome: boldione and three metabolites were detected, with maximum excretion rates after 1.8 hours for boldione and 3.6 hours for the boldenone case; adverse events: not verified in reviewed source; key limitation: single-volunteer urinary pharmacokinetic/analytical study with no clinical efficacy endpoint. [490] (3) Metabolism study: design: administration of boldione to a human volunteer followed by mass-spectrometric urinary metabolite characterization and isotope-ratio analysis; population/sample: a male volunteer; exact sample size not verified in reviewed source; dose: not verified in reviewed source; duration: not verified in reviewed source; comparator: not verified in reviewed source; outcome: boldione was transformed to boldenone, previously described and additional metabolites were detected, and isotope-ratio mass spectrometry was used to assess synthetic versus endogenous origin; adverse events: not verified in reviewed source; key limitation: some metabolite structures were postulated from mass-spectrometric analyses, and the study addresses metabolism/detection rather than clinical benefit. [491]",
